body:
I did some more concrete tests so that we can be a little more confident than "noticeable impact" with respect to CPU usage, so I'll summarize what I believe to be the underlying issue in better detail and then present my data here so it can be considered during review.

### Context
When DisplayServerX11 is created, we call `events_thread.start(_poll_events_thread, this);`, which then calls the following function:
```C++
void DisplayServerX11::_poll_events() {
	while (!events_thread_done.is_set()) {
		_wait_for_events();

		// Process events from the queue.
		{
			MutexLock mutex_lock(events_mutex);

			_check_pending_events(polled_events);
		}
	}
}
```
### The Problem
At the end of `_wait_for_events()`, we have this section:
```C++
struct timeval tv;
tv.tv_usec = 0;
tv.tv_sec = 1;

// Wait for next event or timeout.
int num_ready_fds = select(x11_fd + 1, &in_fds, nullptr, nullptr, &tv);

if (num_ready_fds > 0) {
// Event received.
return true;
} else {
// Error or timeout.
if (num_ready_fds < 0) {
	ERR_PRINT("_wait_for_events: select error: " + itos(errno));
}
return false;
}
```

In theory, this means that whenever a new event becomes available, if the events thread is inside the call to `select()`, it will immediately return, lock `events_mutex`, and call `_check_pending_events(polled_events)` to add the new events to the polled_events vector.

In practice though, what I believe is happening is that sometimes an event becomes available, but before the events thread can wake up, return, and lock the mutex, the main thread can lock the mutex first from `process_events()`, meaning the event that woke up the events thread, (and all events from that time until the main thread releases the lock) are missed by the main thread and have to get processed on the next frame.

One of the ways this delayed processing of events manifests is in "jumpy" relative mouse movement, which is what we see in the first video attachment above.

### The Solution
Because this issue depends on the main thread locking the mutex from `process_events()`, which is called once per frame, it's framerate dependent. This is supported by the fact that (at least on every machine I've tested) lowering the timeout to something just below 16,666 microseconds (1,000,000μs / 60fps) almost completely removes the stuttering from the MRP of #113188 on a 60hz monitor, but at 144hz, the same timeout results in constant stuttering.

This makes sense because if the events thread is only sleeping for the length of just under a single frame, even if it begins that sleep immediately after the main thread unlocks the mutex in `process_events()`, it should wake up and lock the mutex before the main thread has a chance to. The extra margin necessary to fully eliminate the issue is likely because of the time it takes for the events thread to wake up from the blocking call to `select()`, mainly due to context switching and scheduling latency.

Now, to measure the impact that changing the timeout value has on CPU utilization, I wrote a small script to measure a process' CPU usage using `psutil`'s `Process.cpu_percent()` function with an interval of 1 second, measured 30 times and averaged. I then recompiled the engine with various different timeout values and ran the script on the editor process and the running project process of that issue's MRP, once with the process' window focused, and once unfocused.

Here's the results of that experiment in a bar plot:

<img width="1600" height="1000" alt="cpu_usage_unscaled_plot" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c4cf46a3-2d4c-4e62-9350-f696cb3925fe" />

<img width="1600" height="1000" alt="cpu_usage_scaled_plot" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/eb563bbd-01ee-49df-a3f7-b040ee9b8468" />

Specs used during the tests: `Godot v4.6.dev (d5809fe39) - Ubuntu 24.04.3 LTS 24.04 on X11 - X11 display driver, Multi-window, 1 monitor - Vulkan (Forward+) - integrated Intel(R) Iris(R) Xe Graphics (ADL GT2) - 12th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i5-1240P (16 threads) - 15.33 GiB memory`

On the left is the current behaviour which is a timeout of 1 second, and on the right is the timeout removed entirely, allowing the events thread to poll as fast as it can, which is why the usage goes above 100% in the unscaled plot, because the events thread is maxed out at 100% and the main thread is accounting for the rest.

This PR implements the middle option, as I think it's the best balance between addressing the issue (Should completely eliminate the issue up to ~240fps), and avoiding excessive CPU usage.
